bean_Side_of_Panama:_Portobelo-San_Lorenzo"}, {"title": "Fortifications on the Caribbean Side of Panama: Portobelo-San Lorenzo", "url": "https://military-history.fandom.com/wiki/Fortifications_on_the_Caribbean_Side_of_Panama:_Portobelo-San_Lorenzo"}, {"title": "Spanish fortifications in America", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_fortifications_in_America"}, {"title": "Chapter 13", "url": "https://sir-harry-morgan.blogspot.com/2021/01/chapter-13-raid-on-portobello.html"}, {"title": "6 Actions", "url": "https://www.alisonstarr.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/6-Actions-3-Artists-Fort-San-Lorenzo.pdf"}, {"title": "Portobelo", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portobelo"}]}, "The search turned up consistent accounts of the three forts and Drake's death, though some details conflict.\n\n- **Drake's death:** Drake died of dysentery in January 1596 after his final raid. He was buried at sea in a lead coffin near Portobelo, and Drake's 1595 repulse there is also reported. After Francis Drake's final raid, carried out just before his death from dysentery in January 1596, the port was well protected by three separate fortresses bristling with 60 cannons in all. Sir Francis Drake (c. 1540–1596) was buried at sea in a lead coffin just off the coast.\n- **The three forts:** One source names them as San Felipe de Todo Fierro (the Iron Fort) at the harbour entrance, Santiago de la Gloria across the harbour, and San Gerónimo, which was further in towards land and partially under construction. Another says Fort San Felipe was built first at the entrance to the harbour and was fortified with 35 canons.\n- **Morgan's 1668 raid:** Morgan's men took the castles, and the defences were weaker than their reputation. One source says the defenders had only out-of-date cannons and limited ammunition. Afterward, the brass guns, some 57 in total from all the castles were collected and loaded aboard the ships.\n- **Conflicts to note:** Sources disagree on the ransom (one gives 100,000 pesos, another 250,000 pesos including loot). One source says San Gerónimo was designed by Italian engineer Battista Antonelli (1547–1616) but construction didn't begin until 1664. It wasn't completed when Henry Morgan attacked the city four years later. That suggests the forts were not all fully finished in 1668, which conflicts with the \"three forts with 60 cannons\" figure. Wikipedia also dates the English capture of Portobelo to 1601, under William Parker, rather than to Morgan's 1668 raid."], "durationSeconds": 8.491524083999996, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01STrBxQ9t8u85HtznbW5R5B", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
